# Earshot

**Tap each face. Every voice gets its own captions, under the right face.**

Deaf and hard-of-hearing people have a name for being left out of group conversations: *dinner table syndrome*. Single-stream captions turn a table into one stream of text, and you can't tell who said what.

Earshot takes a recorded video of the table. You tap each face once, and:

1. **SAM 3.1 tracks each person** you tapped through the video;
2. **each voice binds to its face automatically.** Muse Voice Transcribe diarizes the audio; Earshot correlates each anonymous voice with mouth movement inside each SAM 3.1 mask and assigns voices to faces. There is no manual matching, and bindings with low confidence are flagged for a one-tap correction;
3. **speech bubbles appear above whoever is talking**, with "Both talking" when turns overlap;
4. **Muse Spark splits side conversations into threads**;
5. **"What did I miss?" runs locally on Muse Glimmer**, so the conversation text stays on this computer. Every summary sentence cites the captions it came from and must pass deterministic citation, name and number checks; otherwise the cited captions are shown word for word;
6. **Spanish bubbles** (Muse Spark), positioned and coloured WebVTT, and speaker-labelled SRT.

**Headline result (public AMI meeting, far-field room mic, 3 repeats):** automatic binding chose **exactly the hand-matched voice-to-face mapping in 3 of 3 runs**, with **0 correction taps**, so **0.919** of caption words landed under the right person, the same as matching by hand. On a second, harder window it scored 0.547 against 0.693 for hand-matching; one flagged tap fixed it in 2 of 3 runs, and the third already matched. **On an independent meeting (ES2008b), it failed a pass rule written before the run:** 0.348 against 0.549 by hand, 2 taps every time, and one wrong binding was not flagged. Treat binding as a strong first guess that you check, not a guarantee. The failure traced to poor diarization of the single far-field mic: **with an 8-mic array recording diarized through classical delay-and-sum, binding chose exactly the hand-matched mapping in 9 of 9 repeats across all three windows, with 0 taps** (exploratory, on windows seen before). **That did not hold on a held-out meeting (ES2008c):** a pre-committed test failed, with binding 0.155 below hand-matching, although one correction tap reached the hand-matched mapping in all three repeats. **Two more held-out meetings:** ES2008d failed badly (0.197 against 0.616), while **IS1009a, with new people in a different room, camera rig and mic array, passed: binding matched hand-matching (0.424) with at most one tap in every repeat.** Five pre-registered attempts to let Muse Spark replace the correction tap all failed. Full numbers, including what didn't work, are in [Results](#results).

